def uid(n): return f'00000000-0000-4000-8000-{n:012d}'
def ref(n): return dict(artifactId=uid(n), sha256=f'{n:064x}', mediaType='application/json', schemaVersion='2.0', sizeBytes=1024)
def save(name, obj):
    body = json.dumps(obj, indent=2) + '\n'
    (EX / name).write_text(body)
    return body.encode()
def content_ref(n, body, media='application/json'):
    out = ref(n)
    out.update(sha256=hashlib.sha256(body).hexdigest(), sizeBytes=len(body), mediaType=media)
    return out


sections = [
 ('intent_scope','Intent and scope','Customers cancel their own unfulfilled orders; refunds are excluded.',
  'Allow an authenticated customer to cancel their own unfulfilled order while preserving the audit history. Repeated requests must return the same cancellation result. A fulfilled order and an order belonging to another customer cannot be cancelled.\n\nThe accepted outcome is a reviewed PR with independent fixture evidence. Refund execution, UI redesign, merge and production deployment are excluded. Criteria C1–C4 define this slice.'),
 ('current_state','Current state','Reuse the existing order, identity and inventory-event contracts.',
  'The fictional fixture has an Orders API, an order database and an inventory-event worker. Identity already supplies the customer subject; inventory accepts an idempotent release event. Pin the repository base to ' + 'a'*40 + ', the v1 order contract and fixture data version before execution.\n\nThe baseline suite must pass except a separately recorded preexisting defect. An unavailable dependency is infrastructure failure, not evidence of a missing feature.\n\n### ContextManifest (fictional discovery record)\n\nSources consulted:\n- Orders API, identity checks and transaction code at repository base aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa: establish the existing ownership and atomic-write boundaries.\n- Order and inventory-event contracts v1, fixture data v1: confirm request/event shapes and the existing consumer dedupe contract.\n- Synthetic incident corpus v1, covering 1–30 September 2026: check prior cancellation/retry failures. No match above the fixture threshold; no past diagnosis is adopted as evidence.\n\nRemaining gaps:\n- Nonblocking: production rollout thresholds are not supplied; this PR-only slice grants no deployment authority and a future release must qualify them.\n- Nonblocking: fixture timing does not establish production latency; preserve existing guardrails and attach before/after fixture measurements.\n\nAssumptions: the fixture database supports the proposed transaction/uniqueness constraints, and the inventory consumer honors stable event identities. S2/S6/S7 must prove these behaviors; a failed assumption reopens design review. There are no blocking discovery gaps in this fictional plan.\n\nThis prose illustrates the ContextManifest content; its structured schema and runtime validation remain W-109 deliverables, not yet proven.'),
 ('design','Design and alternatives','Commit cancellation, audit and outbox intent atomically.',
  'On cancellation, lock the order and validate ownership/state in one transaction. Write the cancellation result, one audit event and one outbox record atomically. Replay returns the original result. The worker releases inventory using the stable cancellation event ID.\n\nA synchronous inventory call was rejected because a remote timeout would leave cancellation and stock state ambiguous. The outbox adds retry/operational work but makes the local decision durable. Inventory release remains asynchronous; the API never claims that stock release has completed.'),
 ('interfaces_data','Interfaces and data','One endpoint and additive records; preserve existing consumers.',
  'POST /orders/{id}/cancel uses authenticated customer identity and an idempotency key. Success returns the stable cancellation result. Foreign orders use the existing nondisclosing access response; fulfilled orders return a conflict. Reusing a key for a different request is a conflict.\n\nAdd a unique cancellation identity, audit event and outbox record. A unique logical order-cancellation constraint and transaction locking prevent distinct keys from cancelling the same order twice. Preserve the current order schema and event consumer compatibility; no column removal or contract narrowing.'),
 ('implementation','Implementation and worktrees','One repo, two components, a bounded integration sequence.',
  'Create isolated task worktrees at the pinned real base. Scope changes to services/orders, workers/inventory-events, the additive migration and relevant test/docs paths. Shared-file changes require review.\n\nPrerequisite: the criteria-author stage has already produced the independently reviewed canonical acceptance package before implementation starts.\n\n1. Add transaction/schema constraints and cancellation handler.\n2. Bind the existing inventory release worker to the stable event identity.\n3. Run the accepted canonical package in independent verification.\n4. Integrate the exact candidate, review and publish the PR.\n\nDo not overwrite the default branch. Reverify if the integration base moves. Reserve the accepted task budget before each unit; an exhausted allowance produces incomplete, not an unchecked PR.'),
 ('acceptance_tests','Acceptance and test strategy','Four criteria, seven concrete scenarios; all are still planned.',
  'C1 covers valid cancellation and rejection of an already fulfilled order. C2 covers replay and simultaneous distinct requests. C3 covers ownership denial. C4 covers durable audit history. Dependency outage recovery also exercises C1/C2.\n\nS1–S7 below specify the exact expected outcomes. Use isolated synthetic customers/orders and a controllable inventory stub. Run the API and database together for transaction assertions; exercise the worker for recovery. Preserve existing contract/regression tests. Execute in a separate verifier checkout with authenticated discovery/execution counts. Run the canonical acceptance controller outside the candidate process against the isolated fixture API; private cases remain outside candidate access. Evidence labels distinguish in-process integrity checks from out-of-process behavior. Enhanced assurance is required by the new API, authenticated ownership checks and persistent migration. Independently accept the oracle before implementation and run fresh adversarial authorization, migration and retry tests against the exact candidate; these remain planned, not yet proven. Feature baseline red is useful but not mandatory; no planned scenario is a passing result.'),
 ('quality_security','Quality and security','Prove ownership checks and at-most-once effects under retries.',
  'Customer identity must come from the authenticated context, not an arbitrary request body. Queries must scope every read/write to that identity and avoid leaking foreign order existence. Keep customer data out of logs.\n\nS3 tests forbidden access, S2/S6 test duplicate and concurrent attempts, and S7 tests recovery after an inventory outage. Existing service latency/resource guardrails remain in force; run the same fixture workload before and after. No new UI is included, so interaction/accessibility changes are not applicable to this API-only slice.'),
 ('delivery_recovery','Delivery and recovery','PR only; include a future rollout and recovery note.',
  'Deliver the exact verified candidate and migration as a PR using completion policy review_completed: remain available for up to three review rounds until required human approvals match the current head. Close or handover before that outcome is incomplete. No merge, preview or production authority is implied. Include a rollout note: additive schema first, compatible worker/handler next, staged enablement after release approval.\n\nOn rollout failure, disable new cancellations and keep draining acknowledged outbox work. Do not reverse already completed business cancellations or discard events by dropping the new table. Preserve audit data and use a reviewed roll-forward repair if data changes preclude rollback. A future release must qualify this procedure independently.'),
 ('operations','Operations and ownership','Watch outbox age, failed retries and duplicate effects.',
  'Correlate request, cancellation, outbox and inventory-operation IDs. Emit cancellation outcomes, replay counts, worker retry counts and oldest-pending-event age without customer PII. Update the application runbook with an inspected-event recovery procedure.\n\nThe Orders owner owns the local transaction; the inventory owner owns consumer compatibility. Record log/report refs and dispose of fixture resources under the task lease. Retain source and evidence for the configured audit period.'),
 ('risks_decisions','Risks and decisions','Concurrent requests and uncertain inventory delivery need explicit checks.',
  'R1 is duplicate cancellation/release during a race. The proposed mitigation is transaction locking, a unique cancellation identity and stable consumer dedupe; S2/S6 must demonstrate the outcome.\n\nR2 is a durable cancellation with inventory temporarily unavailable. The proposed mitigation is outbox retry and operational visibility; S7 must demonstrate eventual single release. These are planned mitigations awaiting implementation evidence. Any unsupported consumer assumption reopens design review before implementation proceeds.'),
 ('definition_of_done','Definition of done','Outcome, evidence, review and scoped delivery all have predicates.',
  'All four criteria must pass the seven planned scenarios and the affected regression/contract matrix on the exact integrated candidate. Canonical test integrity, expected scenario counts and verifier provenance must pass. Enhanced assurance also requires independent oracle acceptance before implementation and a passing fresh adversarial evaluation of the exact candidate.\n\nPeer plan approval and code review must be current; blocking findings must be resolved. Update the API/runbook notes. Reconcile required human PR approvals on the exact head, then publish an outcome manifest and recorded resource cleanup/ownership. Implementation checkboxes alone cannot establish completion; DoD D1–D7 provide the evidence mapping.'),
 ('review_ownership','Review and ownership','Independent primary peer plus the inventory contract owner.',
  'The example author is principal 200. Principal 201 is the independent primary engineering peer covering all sections. Principal 202 is the inventory specialist covering design, interfaces/data, tests, delivery/recovery and risks. Both must approve this exact packet.\n\nThe values are fictional and no live approval is present. The primary cannot substitute for the specialist; neither can self-approve an authored plan. AI findings are advisory. Material changes create a new subject and require all assigned peers to reconfirm, with changed sections emphasized; unchanged retries reuse the current decisions.')
]
